CHAPTER VI
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 43
(1) The community may participate in the implementation of protection for women and children victims of acts of violence.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes among others:
a. establishing family partners at the kalurahan level;
b. establishing community institutions whose field of work is in the protection of women and children;
c. conducting independent socialization of the rights of women and children;
d. undertaking preventive measures if indications of violence against women and children are found in the community;
e. providing first aid to women and children victims of violence; and
f. reporting to the competent authority if violence against women and children occurs in their environment.
(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, community institutions, educational institutions, religious institutions, and the business sector.

Article 30
(1) UPT PPA in carrying out its duties as referred to in Article 29 performs service functions including:
a. public complaints;
b. victim outreach;
c. case management;
d. temporary shelter;
e. mediation; and/or
f. victim assistance.
(2) UPT PPA provides services as referred to in paragraph (1) based on minimum service standards that have been established by laws and regulations.

Article 39
The Kapanewon FPKK as referred to in Article 32 paragraph (2) letter b shall at minimum consist of elements of:
a. health;
b. education;
c. law;
d. psychosocial;
e. Community;
f. spirituality;
g. children's forum; and/or
h. empowerment.

Article 44
(1) Regional Governments involve the business sector and private parties to participate in the protection of women and children.
(2) Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:
a. socialization to employees in the business environment regarding the rights of women and children;
b. socialization to employees in the business environment regarding the dangers of acts of violence against women and children;
c. providing assistance in the implementation of protection of women and children as a form of corporate social responsibility.

